Microcapsules have received considerable attentions and interests in both academic and industrial research fields owing to their attractive properties such as large specific surface area, low effective density, permeability shell and accommodation of guest molecules.[1, 2] As a result, microcapsules have played vital roles in the applications of adsorption and separation,[3, 4] drug delivery,[5] encapsulation,[6] and so on.[7, 8] Up to now, the shells of most microcapsules are made of polymers due to their mechanically flexibility. However, the low thermal stability and chemical incompatibility with inorganic materials naturally limit their use. More notably, nearly all microcapsules possess enclosed structures with one exposed surface to the best of our knowledge. The enclosed structures and intact shells will cause a low mass transfer rate for the substances.[9] Designing and constructing microcapsules with open-mouthed structures (such as bowl-liked, Janus structures) has been regarded as a desired strategy to meet this requirement.[10] The open-mouthed structures could be beneficial for the mass diffusion and fully exposing inner surfaces, thus greatly improving the performance of microcapsules. Nevertheless, only a few examples have been reported to construct open-mouthed microcapsules through a hard-template method to date.[11-13] Moreover, the main drawbacks of this method are the multiple time-consuming steps and harsh conditions for the template-removing. Therefore, the development of facile and novel synthetic procedure to construct monodisperse microcapsules with open-mouthed structures is still a great challenge.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are constructed by coordination bonds between metal ions and organic ligands,[14] have been extensively investigated in the applications of adsorption,[15] gas storage,[16] catalysis,[17] and so on.[18, 19] They are considered as a sort of ideal crystalline material in that all metal clusters and organic linkers are fixed at the designated positions in a periodical lattice. MOFs have shown potential in the synthesis of microcapsules due to the uniform pores in molecular dimension,[20] but thus far, few researches have focused on the synthesis of MOF-based microcapsules,[21, 22] especially for the open-mouthed structures. These may be ascribed to two aspects: (1) The rigidity of reticular structures limits the deformation of MOF crystals; (2) The weak mechanical stability of MOFs prevents the formation of free-standing MOF microcapsules.[23-25] As we know, the rigidity of MOF structures could be weakened through partially destructing the reticular structures of MOFs or decreasing the connectivity of metal clusters.[26] This seems to be a feasible idea to synthesize openmouthed MOF-based microcapsules. In previous works, we have proposed a competitive coordination strategy to construct a series of hollow MOFs through introducing the polyoxometalates (POMs) into synthetic systems.[27, 28] The POM anions would compete with organic ligands to coordinate with metal clusters during the formation of MOFs, thus leading to the hollow structures. Various characterizations have verified the presence of host-guest supramolecular interaction between POMs and metal ions. The POMs partially substitute the organic ligands and present in the final MOF structures.
Herein, for the first time, we demonstrate an improved competitive coordination strategy to synthesize uniform and monodisperse MOF-based bowl-liked microcapsule (MBM) through a facile solvothermal reaction. POMs and sulphur source are added into reaction system simultaneously. When the concentration of them is precisely controlled, the anions generate competitive relationship with organic ligands to partially break the infinite structures and weaken the rigidity of MOF reticular structures, thus resulting in the formation of MBM. Taking advantage of composition and unique bowl-liked structure of MBM, the MOF-based microcapsules are used as useful adsorbents to enrich and remove radioactive iodine which are liberated during nuclear accidents and nuclear fuel treatment.[29]
The MBM were synthesized through a solvothermal reaction of Cobalt (III) acetylacetonate $[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}]$ , 1,3,5- benzenetricarboxylic acid (H3BTC), hexaammonium molybdate $[(N H_{4})_{6}M_{0_{7}}\mathsf{O}_{24}{\bullet}4\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}]$ , hexamethylenetetramine (HMTA), thioacetamide (TAA) and polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP) at $180~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 12h. Unlike most of conventional polymer microcapsules with enclosed sphere structure, the MBM exhibited a novel bowl-liked structure. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM, Figure 1a) showed that the uniform microcapsules possessed hollow cavities with a diameter of approximately $300\ \mathsf{n m}$ and shell thickness of about $50~\mathsf{n m}$ . Rough surface could be observed by the high-magnification TEM image (Figure 1b). The uniform bowl-liked structures were further confirmed through scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Figure 1c, d) and high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) images (Figure 1e, f). The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of the MBM (Figure S1) showed an obvious broad peak assigned to $(2,-1,0)$ , which was consistent with the reported M-BTC MOFs $M=N i$ or Co).[30] The absence of some peaks might be ascribed to the nature of bowl-liked structure and the low crystallinity. Fourier transform infrared spectrum (FT-IR) of MBM was recorded and depicted in Figure S2, the stretching frequency of carboxylate red shifted to 1600 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ from 1700 cm−1 compared to uncoordinated ${\sf H}_{3}{\sf B}{\sf T}{\sf C}_{\mathrm{:}}$ , revealing that the H3BTC units presented in the bowl-liked structure and coordinated with metal ions.[31] Elemental mapping spectra (Figure 1g) and line-scanning spectra (Figure 1h) using energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) demonstrated that the elements C, N, O, S, Co and Mo were well-dispersed in the whole bowl-liked structures. The relative content of elements were determined by C, H, O, N, S elemental analysis and inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICPOES), which deduced a formula of $\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{M o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{5}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}(\mathsf{T A A})_{3}(\mathsf{H M T A})_{2}(\mathsf{D M F})_{2}(\mathsf{E t O H})_{5}$ (Table S1). The oxidative states of elements were determined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The valence of Co and Mo was $^{+2}$ and $^{+6}$ , respectively (Figure S3). Nitrogen adsorption– desorption isotherms were measured at $77~\mathsf{K}$ to discuss the porosity of MBM. As shown in Figure 1i, MBM exhibited a typical type IV isotherm suggesting the presence of mesopores. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area and total pore volume was calculated to be $62\ m^{2}\ |\mathfrak{g}^{1}$ and $0.624\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Pore-size distributions calculated from the SaitoFoley method (Figure 1i, top inset) and Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH, Figure 1i, bottom inset) method showed that both micropores (approximately $1.2\quad\mathsf{n m})$ and mesopores (approximately $4.0~\mathsf{n m})$ existed in the bowl-liked structures simultaneously. Based on the above results, it could be concluded that the skeleton structure of Co-BTC MOF was maintained in MBM, the molybdate and sulfur were doped in the structures.
To further revealing the possible mechanism involving in the formation of MBM, time-dependent experiments were conducted and the solvothermal process was monitored through TEM and SEM. When the reaction time was shorted to $20~\mathrm{\mathsf{min}}$ the products were consisted of uniform solid spheres with an average diameter of $300~\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure 2a1, 2b1). Elemental analysis and ICP-OES results (Table S1) showed that the chemical formula of solid spheres is C29H36O15N4S0.3Mo0.6Co3. Noticeably, the contents of Mo and S were much lower than the final bowl-liked products. Although low crystallinity of sphere products was presented in the PXRD pattern because of the short reaction time, a weak peak (2, -1, 0) could still be observed (Figure S4). FI-IR result demonstrated strong stretching frequency of carboxylic acid verified the presence of the coordination relationship between cobalt ions and H3BTC in the solid spheres (Figure S5). Prolonging the reaction time to $6\mathrm{~h~}$ TEM image (Figure 2a2) demonstrated that the edge of spheres presented darker contrast as compared to the center, indicating that hollow structures began to appear in the spheres. Careful observation of SEM image (Figure 2b2) showed that the surface of spheres started to concave. Perfect and uniform bowl-liked structures with an average cavity diameter of $300~\mathsf{n m}$ and shell thickness of $50~\mathsf{n m}$ were ultimately obtained after a $12\ n$ solvothermal treatment (Figure 2a3, 2b3). Further prolonging the reaction time to $18\mathrm{~h~}$ , no significant change about the size and structure was observed (Figure S6), revealing that the reaction was completed. Furthermore, the concentration of molybdate and TAA had a significant impact on the formation of bowl-liked structures. The MBM were achieved with 9 mg $(N H_{4})_{6}M O_{7}{\sf O}_{24}{\bullet}4{\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}$ and 5 mg TAA at $180~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 12h. Hollow nanospheres were obtained when 3 mg $(N H_{4})_{6}M_{0_{7}}O_{24}{\bullet}4H_{2}O$ was used (Figure S7), while increasing the amount of molybdate to $18\ m{\mathfrak{g}}$ led to the formation of collapsed nanobowls (Figure S8). It seemed that the rigidity of shells could not support the bowl-liked structures under this condition. On the other hand, as the amount of TAA was $2\ m{\mathfrak{g}}$ , most of the products were still solid sphere structures and only a handful of hollow spheres and nanobowls were observed in TEM image (Figure S9) even after 36 h solvothermal treatment. The reaction with 20 mg TAA yielded a large number of irregular particles (Figure S10) which might be some sulfides.
Based on the all above phenomenon, the formation of the bowl-liked structures probably underwent an interesting dissolution-regrowth process which molybdate and TAA had participated in. At first, Co ions coordinated with H3BTC to rapidly form the Co-BTC skeleton structures (solid spheres) due to the strong coordination interactions. Very less molybdate and S element were doped into products during this process. This was confirmed by various characterization results of solidsphere products after 20 min. Subsequently, the inner core began to dissolve gradually resulting from thermodynamically unstability of the fast formed Co-MOF nanosphere.[32] The outward ion diffusion was faster than inward ion diffusion. As a result, the unbalanced diffusion led to the formation of hollow interiors. [33] As we known, there were host-guest supramolecular interactions between metal ions and POM (molybdate).[34] On one hand, the molybdate anions generated electrostatic interaction with Co cations.[35] On the other hand, the oxygen atoms of molybdate acting as electron donors coordinated with Co ions.[34] On account of these interactions, molybdate anions had the tendency to partially substitute BTC groups to form CoPOM-BTC supramolecules. The S element of TAA might be participated in this process simultaneously through the interaction between S and Co elements. As a result, the dissolving Co-BTC nanospheres reacted with molybdate anions and S to regrow an outer shell including Co, BTC and competitive reagents (molybdate and S).[27, 28] The infinite reticular structures of MOFs was partially destructed by the competitive reagents, flexibility had been introduced into the rigidity MOF structures, thus leading to the formation of anisotropic bowl-liked structures. Noticeably, the concentration of competitive reagents should be precisely adjusted. Low concentration resulted in the formation of hollow spheres (rigidity dominated), while high concentration led to the collapse of bowlliked structures (flexibility dominated). There was a balance between rigidity and flexibility in the MBM. This was consistent with our above controllable experiment results.
Radionuclide fission products such as $^{129}|$ and $^{131}|$ originated from nuclear waste pollution have become a significant threat to the sustainable development.[34] Developing high-efficiency adsorbents to capture iodine (I2) would be one of useful routes to overcome these issues. Recently, some function materials have been designed and synthesized to adsorbing iodine with high capacity through Y•••I $(Y=N$ , S, O) halogen bonding[36, 37] and charge-transfer interaction between benzene and iodine.[38] Taking account of the structural and elemental advantages of MBM such as unique bowl-liked, hierarchical porosity and rich N, S, O elements, the $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ uptake ability of MBM was investigated. Firstly, MBM were placed under $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ vapor atmosphere at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 2h to investigate their $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ vapor sorption capacity. As depicted in Figure 3a, the color of products changed apparently from grey to almost black. The thermogravimetric (TG) curves of MBM and $\mathsf{I}_{2}@$ MBM were recorded in air (Figure 3b). There was no obvious weight loss below $200~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for MBM sample, while the ${\mathsf{I}}_{2}@{\mathsf{M B M}}$ showed a weight loss of approximately 49 wt $\%$ from $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $200^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , which could be calculated as $0.98\ \mathfrak{g}\ \mathfrak{l}_{2}$ per gram of MBM. Meanwhile, the high desorption temperature revealed the presence of strong interaction between MBM and $l_{2}$ due to the volatility of $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ . To our delight, MBM also demonstrated iodine uptake capacity from solutions. When MBM were added into 20 mM I2/n-hexane solutions, the purple solution became to fade gradually and turned colorless completely after $75\mathrm{\min}$ (Figure 3c). A time-dependent UV/vis adsorption experiment showed that the concentration of $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ decreased along with time to nearly zero after $75\mathrm{\min}$ (Figure 3d). EDX elemental mapping of MBM after adsorbing $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ in $\mathsf{I}_{2}/\mathsf{n}$ -hexane solution demonstrated that the iodine was well-dispersed in the whole bowl-liked structures and the bowl structures were stable during the whole adsorption process (Figure 3g). Energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) analysis result further confirmed the successful uptake of $1_{2}$ (Figure S11). Furthermore, MBM were able to adsorb $1_{2}$ in aqueous solution. It took only 35 min to make $10~\mathsf{m M}$ $I_{2}/H_{2}O$ solution become colorless (Figure 3e, 3f). In order to measure the maximum adsorption of $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ in aqueous, the MBM were immersed in a high concentration aqueous of $K\mathsf{l}_{3}$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . The solid was filtered and the remaining $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ in filtrate was measured by bisulfite starch titration, indicating that the uptake capacity of adsorbed $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ was $0.88~{\mathfrak{g}}/{\mathfrak{g}}$ MBM. In addition to the adsorption, desorption was another key parameter for the practical application of adsorbents. The release of $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ could be triggered rapidly through immersing $\mathsf{I}_{2}@\mathsf{M B M}$ in dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO). The color of DMSO changed to be dark quickly, suggesting the dissolution of $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ (Figure S12). The STEM images of ${\mathsf{I}}_{2}@{\mathsf{M B M}}$ samples after $1_{2}$ release showed that MBM presented excellent stability during adsorption and desorption processes, the bowl-liked structures were maintained after I2 release (Figure S13, S14). Meanwhile, no I2 species were detected for samples after $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ release through EDS analysis, indicating the complete release of $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ from $\mathsf{I}_{2}@\mathsf{M B M}$ (Figure S15, S16). The good $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ uptake ability of MBM could obviously benefit its practical applications in the future.
In conclusion, we have successfully synthesized novel MOFbased bowl-liked microcapsules through a competitive coordination strategy. Controllable experiments exhibit that molybdate and TAA present a competitive relationship with organic ligands to coordinate with Co ions during the dissolutionregrowth process. The competitive reagents partially destroy the infinite structures of Co-BTC MOF to introduce flexibility into the rigidity reticular structures of MOFs. As a result, the hollow spheres (intermediate products) begin to concave, thus lead to the formation of nanobowls. The bowl-liked microcapsules present the ability of enriching and removing radioactive iodine which mainly comes from their intriguing aesthetic architectures and unique properties. The maximum iodine capacity for vapor and aqueous is $_{0.98\mathrm{~\mathfrak{g}~}}$ and $\mathtt{0.88\ 9}$ iodine per gram MBM, respectively. We believe that our strategy demonstrates a great potential in designing and synthesizing MOF-based microcapsules with tunable structures, improved performance and multifunctional applications.
